coronavirus cases at nursing homes are  also spiking again in the first week of  november new cases tied to nursing homes  surpassed  24 000 according to the kovid tracking  project  that's the worst it's been since early  august nursing home fatalities over the  week  top 2500 carter evans now reports from  salt lake city  craig watson visited his mother ray at  the avalon valley rehabilitation center  in salt lake city every sunday  until early march when the facility  closed to visitors  i had no idea i would never see her  again almost five months later  ray tested positive for coronavirus  watson could only say goodbye over  facetime  the night before she passed away what's  your favorite memory of your mom  she was a character i mean my favorite  memory of her was her personality  she was always good and up for anything  and she was just a lot of fun and  i miss that while most facilities have  banned outside visitors  residents are still at risk of being  exposed to the virus through  unknowing caretakers frustrated for us  because we couldn't see her  but there's people from the outside that  are probably more likely exposed than we  are  going in there every day we cannot  create a bubble  around the elderly folks living in  nursing homes  rebecca gurgas studies public health  policy at the university of chicago  in a newly published paper she looked at  six states currently experiencing surges  including utah  as cases in the community rose she found  weekly cases among nursing home staff  more than tripled and resident cases  more than quadrupled from june to  october  we find that community spread is the  largest predictor of which nursing homes  experience cases and then which nursing  home  experience a severe outbreak with lots  of deaths part of the solution includes  rapid testing of staff on site 87  percent of nursing homes now have access  to rapid testing machines  but a kaiser health news study says  concerns about accuracy  and a shortage of testing supplies have  prompted many facilities to continue to  send samples to  laboratories experts believe eliminating  test delays and reducing community  spread  are critical to reducing nursing home  infections  i just think it's common sense to do  everything we can whether you believe  it's political or not political  it just seems common sense to protect  the most vulnerable among us  now the avalon valley rehabilitation  center says it can't comment on  individual patients but it does use  outside contractors  like nurses and therapy staff and their  screening process they say  does meet federal standards they also  say they're thankful for  increased testing capabilities including  that new rapid test machine  gail all right thank you very much  carter
 

